# Cloud agents

Start every subagent with `environment` set to `cloud` and `run_in_background` set to true. That includes swarm workers, arena runners, architect runners, code delegates, and verifiers.

Pass `cloud_base_branch` when the subagent must start from a branch other than the repository default.

Each writer gets its own `cursor/` branch. Do not create a git worktree for that work.

Set `environment` to `local` only when the task needs the user's own machine. That covers a simulator, local IDE state, a transcript that exists only on that machine, or a credential that exists only there.

In `/arena` Phase A step 4, "own output location" means that runner's own cloud agent and its own `cursor/` branch.

A cloud brief stands alone. State the goal, the files in scope, the base branch, and how to tell the work is done.

Every build brief ends with this sentence.

`use /control-app to verify and show me a video and screenshots as proof.`

# Models

Cloud agents cannot read `~/.cursor/rules`. These lines are the pstack model table for this repo. Use Grok models only. If the tool rejects a slug, use the nearest Grok slug and say which one you used.

Read each line as one role, then a colon, then one or more slugs. Split slugs on commas. The role names `feature, refactoring` and `reflect judgment, divergent, synthesizer` contain commas, and each is still one role. Start one subagent per slug. `arena cross-judge pool` is the exception. Pick one slug from that pool, and pick a slug other than the model that wrote the change when the pool contains one.

Mike wants reviewers on a different Grok model from the builder, so a check sees the work differently. Builders use `grok-4.7-xhigh-fast`.

